Revert: d2d1 ear-clip containment must keep blocking on-edge points

Two attempts at tolerancing d2d_ear_clip_contains, both measured regressions in
Premiere against a 396/400 recovery baseline:

  attempt 1  zero each d within tolerance of its edge, before the sign test
             -> 0/232. Zeroing REMOVES the evidence that a point is outside: an
                outside point has mixed signs, and zeroing one collapses it to a
                single sign, which reads as "contained". Every ear blocked.
  attempt 2  keep the sign test, then require the point to be inside by a margin
             -> 0/313, and declining at FOUR vertices remaining with large crosses
                (0.03-1.5) -- well-formed vertices, not slivers. Letting an on-edge
                point through admits an ear that strands it outside the shrinking
                polygon; the corruption accumulates and the tail has no ear at all.

So the function's comment is wrong and its code is right. `!(neg && pos)` reports a
point exactly on an edge as CONTAINED, and that is load-bearing: these polygons are
full of collinear and duplicate points, and blocking those ears is what keeps the
remaining polygon simple as it shrinks. The comment has been replaced with the two
results so this is not attempted a third time.

The four declines a session remain open. They are a real defect -- those geometries
render nothing -- but whatever fixes them, it is not loosening this test.

Kept from the reverted commit, both independently verified:
  * the path-mask guard resolving lazy tessellation before reading face_count
  * NEUTRON_FORCE_EARCLIP=1, which is how the next attempt should be tested first

⚠️ Note the harness does NOT discriminate here: forced, the comb and circle render
correctly under all three versions of the containment test. It catches a dead
fallback, not a subtly wrong one. Premiere's shapes remain the only witness for that,
which is worth fixing before anyone tries again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
@@ -2966,51 +2966,35 @@
 static BOOL d2d_ear_clip_contains(const D2D1_POINT_2F *a, const D2D1_POINT_2F *b,
         const D2D1_POINT_2F *c, const D2D1_POINT_2F *p)
 {
-    double d1, d2, d3, lab, lbc, lca, longest, eps, dist;
+    double d1, d2, d3;
     BOOL neg, pos;
 
     d1 = ((double)p->x - b->x) * ((double)a->y - b->y) - ((double)a->x - b->x) * ((double)p->y - b->y);
     d2 = ((double)p->x - c->x) * ((double)b->y - c->y) - ((double)b->x - c->x) * ((double)p->y - c->y);
     d3 = ((double)p->x - a->x) * ((double)c->y - a->y) - ((double)c->x - a->x) * ((double)p->y - a->y);
 
-    /* 🚨 ...and EXACTLY on the edge is not the test either -- but the FIRST attempt at that got
-     * the logic backwards and is worth recording, because it looked reasonable and destroyed the
-     * whole fallback: zeroing a d to mean "on the edge" REMOVES the evidence that a point is
-     * outside. A point clearly outside has mixed signs; zero one of them and it can collapse to a
-     * single sign, which !(neg && pos) then reads as CONTAINED. Measured effect: recovery went
-     * from 396/400 to 0/232 -- every ear blocked, the fallback dead.
-     *
-     * The tolerance has to be applied to the CONCLUSION, not the inputs. Mixed signs still mean
-     * outside. Otherwise the point is inside or on the boundary, and it should only block the ear
-     * if it is inside by a real margin: |d| for an edge is twice the area of (p, edge), so |d|
-     * divided by that edge's length is p's perpendicular distance from it. Require that distance
-     * to exceed a millionth of the triangle's longest edge.
-     *
-     * This also makes the function do what its own comment always claimed. `!(neg && pos)` returns
-     * CONTAINED for a point exactly on an edge (one d is zero, the others share a sign), so
-     * on-edge vertices were blocking ears despite the comment saying they did not -- which is
-     * precisely what jammed the last four declines a session: fans of curve samples where every
-     * candidate ear has its neighbours sitting on its edges. */
     neg = d1 < 0.0 || d2 < 0.0 || d3 < 0.0;
     pos = d1 > 0.0 || d2 > 0.0 || d3 > 0.0;
 
-    if (neg && pos)
-        return FALSE;               /* unambiguously outside */
-
-    lab = hypot((double)a->x - b->x, (double)a->y - b->y);
-    lbc = hypot((double)b->x - c->x, (double)b->y - c->y);
-    lca = hypot((double)c->x - a->x, (double)c->y - a->y);
-    longest = lab > lbc ? lab : lbc;
-    if (lca > longest) longest = lca;
-    eps = 1e-7 * longest;
-
-    /* perpendicular distance to the nearest of the three edges */
-    dist = DBL_MAX;
-    if (lab > 0.0 && fabs(d1) / lab < dist) dist = fabs(d1) / lab;
-    if (lbc > 0.0 && fabs(d2) / lbc < dist) dist = fabs(d2) / lbc;
-    if (lca > 0.0 && fabs(d3) / lca < dist) dist = fabs(d3) / lca;
-
-    return dist > eps;              /* inside, but only blocks if it is properly inside */
+    /* ⛔ DO NOT "FIX" THIS TO MATCH THE COMMENT BELOW IT. Tried twice, 2026-08-23, both regressions
+     * measured in Premiere against a 396/400 baseline:
+     *
+     *   attempt 1  zero each d within tolerance of its edge, before the sign test
+     *              -> 0/232. Zeroing REMOVES the evidence a point is outside: an outside point has
+     *                 mixed signs, and zeroing one collapses it to a single sign = "contained".
+     *   attempt 2  keep the sign test, then require the point to be inside by a margin
+     *              -> 0/313, and now declining at FOUR vertices remaining with large crosses
+     *                 (0.03-1.5), i.e. well-formed vertices. Letting an on-edge point through
+     *                 admits an ear that strands it outside the shrinking polygon; the corruption
+     *                 accumulates and the tail has no ear at all.
+     *
+     * So the comment is wrong and the code is right: `!(neg && pos)` DOES report a point exactly on
+     * an edge as contained, and that is load-bearing -- these polygons are full of collinear and
+     * duplicate points, and blocking those ears is what keeps the remaining polygon simple.
+     *
+     * The four declines a session this was chasing are a real but separate defect. Whatever fixes
+     * them, it is not loosening this test. */
+    return !(neg && pos);
 }
 
 static BOOL d2d_path_geometry_ear_clip(struct d2d_geometry *geometry,
